{"title":"Synthetic division","text":"<div class=\"mw-content-ltr mw-parser-output\" lang=\"en\" dir=\"ltr\"><p><b>Synthetic division</b> is an <a href=\"/wiki/index.php?title=Algorithm\" title=\"Algorithm\">algorithm</a> for dividing <a href=\"/wiki/index.php?title=Polynomial\" title=\"Polynomial\">polynomials</a> (usually when the divisor is linear and <a href=\"/wiki/index.php?title=Monic\" class=\"mw-redirect\" title=\"Monic\">monic</a> (that is, of the form <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/7/9/b/79b2b7ab0341df3dd3ea02ee6680ae31a947af07.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$x-a$\" width=\"42\" height=\"8\" /> for some number <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/c/7/d/c7d457e388298246adb06c587bccd419ea67f7e8.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$a$\" style=\"vertical-align: -1px\" width=\"11\" height=\"10\" />)). It is generally used as an alternative for other methods, such as the often more tedious <a href=\"/wiki/index.php?title=Polynomial_long_division&amp;action=edit&amp;redlink=1\" class=\"new\" title=\"Polynomial long division (page does not exist)\">polynomial long division</a>.\n</p>\n<div id=\"toc\" class=\"toc\" role=\"navigation\" aria-labelledby=\"mw-toc-heading\"><input type=\"checkbox\" role=\"button\" id=\"toctogglecheckbox\" class=\"toctogglecheckbox\" style=\"display:none\" /><div class=\"toctitle\" lang=\"en\" dir=\"ltr\"><h2 id=\"mw-toc-heading\">Contents</h2><span class=\"toctogglespan\"><label class=\"toctogglelabel\" for=\"toctogglecheckbox\"></label></span></div>\n<ul>\n<li class=\"toclevel-1 tocsection-1\"><a href=\"#A_Brief_Note\"><span class=\"tocnumber\">1</span> <span class=\"toctext\">A Brief Note</span></a></li>\n<li class=\"toclevel-1 tocsection-2\"><a href=\"#Linear_Factors\"><span class=\"tocnumber\">2</span> <span class=\"toctext\">Linear Factors</span></a>\n<ul>\n<li class=\"toclevel-2 tocsection-3\"><a href=\"#Procedure\"><span class=\"tocnumber\">2.1</span> <span class=\"toctext\">Procedure</span></a></li>\n<li class=\"toclevel-2 tocsection-4\"><a href=\"#Another_Example\"><span class=\"tocnumber\">2.2</span> <span class=\"toctext\">Another Example</span></a></li>\n<li class=\"toclevel-2 tocsection-5\"><a href=\"#Non-monic_Factors\"><span class=\"tocnumber\">2.3</span> <span class=\"toctext\">Non-monic Factors</span></a></li>\n<li class=\"toclevel-2 tocsection-6\"><a href=\"#Connection_to_Remainder_Theorem\"><span class=\"tocnumber\">2.4</span> <span class=\"toctext\">Connection to Remainder Theorem</span></a></li>\n<li class=\"toclevel-2 tocsection-7\"><a href=\"#Exercises\"><span class=\"tocnumber\">2.5</span> <span class=\"toctext\">Exercises</span></a></li>\n</ul>\n</li>\n<li class=\"toclevel-1 tocsection-8\"><a href=\"#Non-Linear_Factors\"><span class=\"tocnumber\">3</span> <span class=\"toctext\">Non-Linear Factors</span></a>\n<ul>\n<li class=\"toclevel-2 tocsection-9\"><a href=\"#Quadratic_Factors\"><span class=\"tocnumber\">3.1</span> <span class=\"toctext\">Quadratic Factors</span></a></li>\n<li class=\"toclevel-2 tocsection-10\"><a href=\"#Non-monic_Factors_2\"><span class=\"tocnumber\">3.2</span> <span class=\"toctext\">Non-monic Factors</span></a></li>\n<li class=\"toclevel-2 tocsection-11\"><a href=\"#Higher_Degree_Factors\"><span class=\"tocnumber\">3.3</span> <span class=\"toctext\">Higher Degree Factors</span></a></li>\n<li class=\"toclevel-2 tocsection-12\"><a href=\"#Problems\"><span class=\"tocnumber\">3.4</span> <span class=\"toctext\">Problems</span></a></li>\n</ul>\n</li>\n<li class=\"toclevel-1 tocsection-13\"><a href=\"#See_Also\"><span class=\"tocnumber\">4</span> <span class=\"toctext\">See Also</span></a></li>\n</ul>\n</div>\n\n<h2><span class=\"mw-headline\" id=\"A_Brief_Note\">A Brief Note</span><span class=\"mw-editsection\"><span class=\"mw-editsection-bracket\">[</span><a href=\"/wiki/index.php?title=Synthetic_division&amp;action=edit&amp;section=1\" title=\"Edit section: A Brief Note\">edit</a><span class=\"mw-editsection-bracket\">]</span></span></h2>\n<p>When learning a mathematical procedure such as this one, many practice problems are absolutely essential. This article provides a few exercises, but more will likely be necessary for long-term retention of this information. Fortunately, it is quite easy to come up with two polynomials you would like to divide in this manner.\n</p>\n<h2><span class=\"mw-headline\" id=\"Linear_Factors\">Linear Factors</span><span class=\"mw-editsection\"><span class=\"mw-editsection-bracket\">[</span><a href=\"/wiki/index.php?title=Synthetic_division&amp;action=edit&amp;section=2\" title=\"Edit section: Linear Factors\">edit</a><span class=\"mw-editsection-bracket\">]</span></span></h2>\n<h3><span class=\"mw-headline\" id=\"Procedure\">Procedure</span><span class=\"mw-editsection\"><span class=\"mw-editsection-bracket\">[</span><a href=\"/wiki/index.php?title=Synthetic_division&amp;action=edit&amp;section=3\" title=\"Edit section: Procedure\">edit</a><span class=\"mw-editsection-bracket\">]</span></span></h3>\n<p>Suppose we have some polynomial <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/1/f/4/1f483d5c105a31b2675716facd0f642ae63daae6.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$P(x)=a_nx^n+a_{n-1}x^{n-1}+\\cdots+a_1x+a_0$\" style=\"vertical-align: -5px\" width=\"330\" height=\"21\" /> that we would like to divide by some factor <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/7/9/b/79b2b7ab0341df3dd3ea02ee6680ae31a947af07.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$x-a$\" width=\"42\" height=\"8\" />. Then, we set the <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/c/7/d/c7d457e388298246adb06c587bccd419ea67f7e8.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$a$\" style=\"vertical-align: -1px\" width=\"11\" height=\"10\" /> off to the side and arrange the coefficients of <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/6/e/f/6efaaf74e576202fad965134bc49c6a34aac4c30.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$P(x)$\" style=\"vertical-align: -5px\" width=\"41\" height=\"20\" /> as follows while taking care not to omit coefficients equal to <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/b/c/1/bc1f9d9bf8a1b606a4188b5ce9a2af1809e27a89.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$0$\" style=\"vertical-align: -1px\" width=\"11\" height=\"14\" /> and keeping negative signs:\n<img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/5/5/1/551afc59e9bdec70c6c493a0a37216bd2d5493f5.png\" class=\"latexcenter\" alt=\"[asy] draw((0,0)--(1,0)--(1,1)); draw((2,-2.5)--(11,-2.5)); draw((9.5,1)--(9.5,-3.5),dotted);  label(&quot;$a$&quot;, (1,0), NW); label(&quot;$a_n$&quot;, (2,0), NE); label(&quot;$a_{n-1}$&quot;, (4,0), NE); label(&quot;$\\cdots$&quot;, (6,0), NE); label(&quot;$a_1$&quot;, (8,0), NE); label(&quot;$a_0$&quot;, (10,0), NE); [/asy]\" width=\"252\" height=\"105\" />\n</p><p>For this explanation, we will evaluate <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/e/6/b/e6ba8d436feb64a43807a789bac1ace78915a6e8.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$\\frac{2x^4-6x^3+7x^2+5x-22}{x-2}$\" style=\"vertical-align: -15px\" width=\"213\" height=\"42\" />.\n</p><p>We begin by bringing down the first coefficient:\n</p><p><img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/8/4/4/84442f6b59001f48b5602f2150798210dca3056a.png\" class=\"latexcenter\" alt=\"[asy] draw((0,0)--(1,0)--(1,1)); draw((2,-2.5)--(12,-2.5)); draw((9.5,1)--(9.5,-3.5),dotted);  label(&quot;$2$&quot;, (1,0), NW); label(&quot;$2$&quot;, (2,0), NE); label(&quot;$-6$&quot;, (3.5,0), NE); label(&quot;$7$&quot;, (6,0), NE); label(&quot;$5$&quot;, (8,0), NE); label(&quot;$-22$&quot;, (10,0), NE); label(&quot;$2$&quot;, (2,-3.25), E); [/asy]\" width=\"252\" height=\"98\" />\n</p><p>Now, we multiply the coefficient we just brought down (namely, <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/4/1/c/41c544263a265ff15498ee45f7392c5f86c6d151.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$2$\" style=\"vertical-align: -1px\" width=\"11\" height=\"14\" />) by the number we put in the top left (which also equals <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/4/1/c/41c544263a265ff15498ee45f7392c5f86c6d151.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$2$\" style=\"vertical-align: -1px\" width=\"11\" height=\"14\" />), and then place this product (which equals <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/c/7/c/c7cab1a05e1e0c1d51a6a219d96577a16b7abf9d.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$4$\" style=\"vertical-align: -1px\" width=\"11\" height=\"14\" />) into the next column above the bar:\n</p><p><img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/1/b/6/1b646180d5010ceb05e524a333643bebae5866d2.png\" class=\"latexcenter\" alt=\"[asy] draw((0,0)--(1,0)--(1,1)); draw((2,-2.5)--(12,-2.5)); draw((9.5,1)--(9.5,-3.5),dotted);  label(&quot;$2$&quot;, (1,0), NW); label(&quot;$2$&quot;, (2,0), NE); label(&quot;$-6$&quot;, (3.5,0), NE); label(&quot;$7$&quot;, (6,0), NE); label(&quot;$5$&quot;, (8,0), NE); label(&quot;$-22$&quot;, (10,0), NE);  label(&quot;$4$&quot;, (4, -1.5), E);  label(&quot;$2$&quot;, (2,-3.25), E); [/asy]\" width=\"252\" height=\"98\" />\n</p><p>Then, add this product to the coefficient above it:\n</p><p><img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/3/5/5/35509186ccc6590af063c076c36cd7a64497c07b.png\" class=\"latexcenter\" alt=\"[asy] draw((0,0)--(1,0)--(1,1)); draw((2,-2.5)--(12,-2.5)); draw((9.5,1)--(9.5,-3.5),dotted);  label(&quot;$2$&quot;, (1,0), NW); label(&quot;$2$&quot;, (2,0), NE); label(&quot;$-6$&quot;, (3.5,0), NE); label(&quot;$7$&quot;, (6,0), NE); label(&quot;$5$&quot;, (8,0), NE); label(&quot;$-22$&quot;, (10,0), NE);  label(&quot;$4$&quot;, (4, -1.5), E);  label(&quot;$2$&quot;, (2,-3.25), E); label(&quot;$-2$&quot;, (3.5,-3.25), E); [/asy]\" width=\"252\" height=\"102\" />\n</p><p>Repeat this process (as shown below) until all rows have been filled:\n<img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/c/e/c/cec167982038fcaa2d3d407644828aa4fe8db3dd.png\" class=\"latexcenter\" alt=\"[asy] draw((0,0)--(1,0)--(1,1)); draw((2,-2.5)--(12,-2.5)); draw((9.5,1)--(9.5,-3.5),dotted);  label(&quot;$2$&quot;, (1,0), NW); label(&quot;$2$&quot;, (2,0), NE); label(&quot;$-6$&quot;, (3.5,0), NE); label(&quot;$7$&quot;, (6,0), NE); label(&quot;$5$&quot;, (8,0), NE); label(&quot;$-22$&quot;, (10,0), NE);  label(&quot;$4$&quot;, (4, -1.5), E); label(&quot;$-4$&quot;, (5.5, -1.5), E); label(&quot;$6$&quot;, (8,-1.5), E); label(&quot;$22$&quot;, (10.25,-1.5), E);  label(&quot;$2$&quot;, (2,-3.25), E); label(&quot;$-2$&quot;, (3.5,-3.25), E); label(&quot;$3$&quot;, (6, -3.25), E); label(&quot;$11$&quot;, (7.75, -3.25), E); label(&quot;$0$&quot;, (10.25, -3.25), E); [/asy]\" width=\"252\" height=\"102\" />\n</p><p>Now, the numbers to the left of the dotted line represent the coefficients of the quotient polynomial (which has a <a href=\"/wiki/index.php?title=Degree_of_a_polynomial\" title=\"Degree of a polynomial\">degree</a> one less than that of the dividend), and the rightmost term represents the remainder (which is multiplied by <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/e/5/3/e53eaf8814dbda284beba432a4c6316fecf6aa5d.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$\\tfrac1{x-a}$\" style=\"vertical-align: -8px\" width=\"31\" height=\"24\" /> in the final result).\n</p><p>So, we use the coefficients we derived above to deduce our final answer: \n<img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/e/1/2/e1248d13107c25eb70e83adda47d93fbbc5c0fb0.png\" class=\"latexcenter\" alt=\"\\[\\frac{2x^4-6x^3+7x^2+5x-22}{x-2}=2x^3-2x^2+3x+11+\\frac0{x-2}=\\boxed{2x^3-2x^2+3x+11}.\\]\" width=\"624\" height=\"39\" />\n</p>\n<h3><span class=\"mw-headline\" id=\"Another_Example\">Another Example</span><span class=\"mw-editsection\"><span class=\"mw-editsection-bracket\">[</span><a href=\"/wiki/index.php?title=Synthetic_division&amp;action=edit&amp;section=4\" title=\"Edit section: Another Example\">edit</a><span class=\"mw-editsection-bracket\">]</span></span></h3>\n<p>Now, we will evaluate <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/b/2/2/b229a4de65d9ea53022bfd4c08b304ceaa755019.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$\\frac{x^3-4x+3}{x+5}$\" style=\"vertical-align: -15px\" width=\"97\" height=\"42\" />. We arrange the coefficients as follows while making sure not to forget the <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/7/6/b/76b2878564ab19b80abb21ba964abe90fe120846.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$x^2$\" width=\"16\" height=\"15\" /> term, which has a coefficient of <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/b/c/1/bc1f9d9bf8a1b606a4188b5ce9a2af1809e27a89.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$0$\" style=\"vertical-align: -1px\" width=\"11\" height=\"14\" />:\n</p><p><img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/f/7/c/f7c6784a52878f3ed4fc9f9327ce0eca7a3a578b.png\" class=\"latexcenter\" alt=\"[asy] draw((0,0)--(1,0)--(1,1)); draw((2,-2.5)--(9,-2.5)); draw((7.5,1)--(7.5,-3.5),dotted);  label(&quot;$-5$&quot;, (1,0), NW); label(&quot;$1$&quot;, (2,0), NE); label(&quot;$0$&quot;, (4,0), NE); label(&quot;$-4$&quot;, (5.5,0), NE); label(&quot;$3$&quot;, (8,0), NE); [/asy]\" width=\"252\" height=\"125\" />\n</p><p>Now, we can follow the above procedure to find our desired answer:\n<img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/3/6/1/3619be247187527f52e63a833b9c4581b06b440f.png\" class=\"latexcenter\" alt=\"[asy] draw((0,0)--(1,0)--(1,1)); draw((2,-2.5)--(9,-2.5)); draw((7.5,1)--(7.5,-3.5),dotted);  label(&quot;$-5$&quot;, (1,0), NW); label(&quot;$1$&quot;, (2,0), NE); label(&quot;$0$&quot;, (4,0), NE); label(&quot;$-4$&quot;, (5.5,0), NE); label(&quot;$3$&quot;, (8,0), NE);  label(&quot;$-5$&quot;, (3.5, -1.5), E); label(&quot;$25$&quot;, (5.75, -1.5), E); label(&quot;$-105$&quot;, (7.25, -1.5), E);  label(&quot;$1$&quot;, (2,-3.25), E); label(&quot;$-5$&quot;, (3.5, -3.25), E); label(&quot;$21$&quot;, (5.75, -3.25), E); label(&quot;$-102$&quot;, (7.25, -3.25), E); [/asy]\" width=\"252\" height=\"125\" />\n</p><p>From these numbers, we can deduce our final answer: <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/6/e/e/6ee64910d2cc6909a78cafcb0c9f868aa904a24c.png\" class=\"latexcenter\" alt=\"\\[\\frac{x^3-4x+3}{x+5}=\\boxed{x^2-5x+21-\\frac{102}{x+5}}.\\]\" width=\"299\" height=\"50\" />\n</p>\n<h3><span class=\"mw-headline\" id=\"Non-monic_Factors\">Non-monic Factors</span><span class=\"mw-editsection\"><span class=\"mw-editsection-bracket\">[</span><a href=\"/wiki/index.php?title=Synthetic_division&amp;action=edit&amp;section=5\" title=\"Edit section: Non-monic Factors\">edit</a><span class=\"mw-editsection-bracket\">]</span></span></h3>\n<p>If the divisor of polynomial <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/6/e/f/6efaaf74e576202fad965134bc49c6a34aac4c30.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$P(x)$\" style=\"vertical-align: -5px\" width=\"41\" height=\"20\" /> is linear and non-monic (i.e. of the form <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/b/4/c/b4c370b3079d5a963f49564aca6d579df79a35d9.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$ax-b$\" style=\"vertical-align: -2px\" width=\"52\" height=\"16\" /> for some constants <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/c/7/d/c7d457e388298246adb06c587bccd419ea67f7e8.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$a$\" style=\"vertical-align: -1px\" width=\"11\" height=\"10\" /> and <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/8/1/3/8136a7ef6a03334a7246df9097e5bcc31ba33fd2.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$b$\" style=\"vertical-align: -1px\" width=\"10\" height=\"14\" />), then we can factor out the linear term coefficient to reduce this fraction to a constant times a fraction in which the divisor is monic, as follows: <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/7/0/c/70cd8ffb00635acb87d0fb5aaa96e16551098c2c.png\" class=\"latexcenter\" alt=\"\\[\\frac{P(x)}{ax-b}=\\frac{P(x)}{a(x-\\tfrac ba)}=\\frac1a\\cdot\\frac{P(x)}{x-\\tfrac ba}.\\]\" width=\"250\" height=\"45\" />\n</p><p>For example, <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/1/5/3/15313f11899461ed629f4011f76c8e4e0527129d.png\" class=\"latexcenter\" alt=\"\\[\\frac{x^3+2x+1}{3x-6}=\\frac13\\cdot\\frac{x^3+2x+1}{x-2}.\\]\" width=\"241\" height=\"39\" /> We can now evaluate this expression via the procedure described above.\n</p>\n<h3><span class=\"mw-headline\" id=\"Connection_to_Remainder_Theorem\">Connection to Remainder Theorem</span><span class=\"mw-editsection\"><span class=\"mw-editsection-bracket\">[</span><a href=\"/wiki/index.php?title=Synthetic_division&amp;action=edit&amp;section=6\" title=\"Edit section: Connection to Remainder Theorem\">edit</a><span class=\"mw-editsection-bracket\">]</span></span></h3>\n<p>The <a href=\"/wiki/index.php?title=Polynomial_Remainder_Theorem\" title=\"Polynomial Remainder Theorem\">Polynomial Remainder Theorem</a> states that the remainder when dividing <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/6/e/f/6efaaf74e576202fad965134bc49c6a34aac4c30.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$P(x)$\" style=\"vertical-align: -5px\" width=\"41\" height=\"20\" /> by <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/7/9/b/79b2b7ab0341df3dd3ea02ee6680ae31a947af07.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$x-a$\" width=\"42\" height=\"8\" /> equals <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/f/2/a/f2ac37e8ff9aeea3ac5eaf42779864032eb645c9.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$P(a)$\" style=\"vertical-align: -4px\" width=\"36\" height=\"18\" />. This fact can be used to evaluate values of <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/f/2/a/f2ac37e8ff9aeea3ac5eaf42779864032eb645c9.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$P(a)$\" style=\"vertical-align: -4px\" width=\"36\" height=\"18\" /> often more efficiently than plugging in <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/c/7/d/c7d457e388298246adb06c587bccd419ea67f7e8.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$a$\" style=\"vertical-align: -1px\" width=\"11\" height=\"10\" /> manually. For example, suppose <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/5/4/3/5434fbdeaceb3b9322b89ccc324ec89874479227.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$P(x)=x^6-2x^5+3x^2-80x+1$\" style=\"vertical-align: -5px\" width=\"263\" height=\"21\" />, and we desire to find <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/0/8/8/088dd18aa6232b0bc2892ad50b18794a5c7a1538.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$P(3)$\" style=\"vertical-align: -4px\" width=\"35\" height=\"18\" />. We could plug in <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/7/c/d/7cde695f2e4542fd01f860a89189f47a27143b66.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$3$\" style=\"vertical-align: -1px\" width=\"11\" height=\"14\" /> and calculate this manually, or we could use synthetic division, where we divide <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/6/e/f/6efaaf74e576202fad965134bc49c6a34aac4c30.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$P(x)$\" style=\"vertical-align: -5px\" width=\"41\" height=\"20\" /> by <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/3/7/f/37fb1d324dcbe00e34ae63ab8dad431052fbffd9.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$x-3$\" width=\"41\" height=\"12\" /> and find the remainder:\n</p><p><img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/a/f/2/af2530592dcc63a7c6f1e49b0d2736b3b27ebbcc.png\" class=\"latexcenter\" alt=\"[asy] size(7cm);  draw((0,0)--(1,0)--(1,1)); draw((2,-2.5)--(15,-2.5)); draw((13.5,1)--(13.5,-3.5),dotted);  label(&quot;$3$&quot;, (1,0), NW); label(&quot;$1$&quot;, (2,0), NE); label(&quot;$-2$&quot;, (3.5,0), NE); label(&quot;$0$&quot;, (6,0), NE); label(&quot;$0$&quot;, (8,0), NE); label(&quot;$3$&quot;, (10,0), NE); label(&quot;$-80$&quot;, (11.25,0), NE); label(&quot;$1$&quot;, (14,0), NE);  label(&quot;$3$&quot;, (4, -1.5), E); label(&quot;$3$&quot;, (6, -1.5), E); label(&quot;$9$&quot;, (8, -1.5), E); label(&quot;$27$&quot;, (9.75, -1.5), E); label(&quot;$90$&quot;, (11.75, -1.5), E); label(&quot;$30$&quot;, (13.75, -1.5), E);  label(&quot;$1$&quot;, (2,-3.25), E); label(&quot;$1$&quot;, (4, -3.25), E); label(&quot;$3$&quot;, (6, -3.25), E); label(&quot;$9$&quot;, (8, -3.25), E); label(&quot;$30$&quot;, (9.75, -3.25), E); label(&quot;$10$&quot;, (11.75, -3.25), E); label(&quot;$31$&quot;, (13.75, -3.25), E); [/asy]\" width=\"332\" height=\"105\" />\n</p><p>Thus, because our remainder equals <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/2/9/6/296fac336caaa4265aa24b6ecbc408a348ad7e0b.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$31$\" style=\"vertical-align: -1px\" width=\"20\" height=\"14\" />, <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/a/0/4/a0409fe605ff2acaca6e6e5e92c4d310fc475579.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$P(3)=\\boxed{31}$\" style=\"vertical-align: -6px\" width=\"93\" height=\"25\" /> by the Remainder Theorem.\n</p>\n<h3><span class=\"mw-headline\" id=\"Exercises\">Exercises</span><span class=\"mw-editsection\"><span class=\"mw-editsection-bracket\">[</span><a href=\"/wiki/index.php?title=Synthetic_division&amp;action=edit&amp;section=7\" title=\"Edit section: Exercises\">edit</a><span class=\"mw-editsection-bracket\">]</span></span></h3>\n<ol><li>Show that <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/8/9/f/89fb27ae3a8014b63a0e4e411a0a6e8b776b73ce.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$\\frac{3x^5-2x^2+3x+8}{2x+2}=\\frac12(3x^4-3x^3+3x^2-5x+8)$\" style=\"vertical-align: -15px\" width=\"405\" height=\"42\" />.</li>\n<li>Evaluate <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/9/6/6/966ffa9992e8f1d3dfbee0801f863e69ebf19fd7.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$\\frac{z^4-3z^3+6z-4}{z-3}$\" style=\"vertical-align: -15px\" width=\"142\" height=\"42\" />.</li>\n<li>Evaluate <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/d/7/9/d798d965708bff55de81596da1ab956e6b4c9390.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$\\frac{(y+3)^2-5}{y-4}$\" style=\"vertical-align: -17px\" width=\"100\" height=\"44\" />.</li>\n<li>Suppose <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/c/d/a/cda2dc0626cebeaca0c50bd8046c41f6e3e7246d.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$P(x)=3x^4+2x^3-80x^2-10x+9$\" style=\"vertical-align: -5px\" width=\"281\" height=\"21\" />. Without plugging into this function, find <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/7/5/d/75d2840f4c4a51ffb604727078a50a895034a531.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$P(5)$\" style=\"vertical-align: -4px\" width=\"35\" height=\"18\" />. Then, check your answer by plugging in <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/7/9/0/79069377f91364c2f87a64e5f9f562a091c8a6c1.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$5$\" style=\"vertical-align: -1px\" width=\"11\" height=\"14\" /> manually.</li></ol>\n<h2><span class=\"mw-headline\" id=\"Non-Linear_Factors\">Non-Linear Factors</span><span class=\"mw-editsection\"><span class=\"mw-editsection-bracket\">[</span><a href=\"/wiki/index.php?title=Synthetic_division&amp;action=edit&amp;section=8\" title=\"Edit section: Non-Linear Factors\">edit</a><span class=\"mw-editsection-bracket\">]</span></span></h2>\n<h3><span class=\"mw-headline\" id=\"Quadratic_Factors\">Quadratic Factors</span><span class=\"mw-editsection\"><span class=\"mw-editsection-bracket\">[</span><a href=\"/wiki/index.php?title=Synthetic_division&amp;action=edit&amp;section=9\" title=\"Edit section: Quadratic Factors\">edit</a><span class=\"mw-editsection-bracket\">]</span></span></h3>\n<p>Suppose we have some polynomial <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/6/e/f/6efaaf74e576202fad965134bc49c6a34aac4c30.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$P(x)$\" style=\"vertical-align: -5px\" width=\"41\" height=\"20\" /> that we would like to divide by <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/f/a/8/fa883413b28d855a12d1a1d07ee597f5274cdf46.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$x^2-bx-c$\" style=\"vertical-align: -2px\" width=\"90\" height=\"18\" />. Then, we can place <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/8/1/3/8136a7ef6a03334a7246df9097e5bcc31ba33fd2.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$b$\" style=\"vertical-align: -1px\" width=\"10\" height=\"14\" /> and <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/3/3/7/3372c1cb6d68cf97c2d231acc0b47b95a9ed04cc.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$c$\" style=\"vertical-align: -1px\" width=\"10\" height=\"10\" /> along a diagonal along the side and arrange the coefficients of <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/6/e/f/6efaaf74e576202fad965134bc49c6a34aac4c30.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$P(x)$\" style=\"vertical-align: -5px\" width=\"41\" height=\"20\" /> as usual, as seen in the example below.\n</p><p>Now, we will evaluate <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/6/1/e/61ee78bbe58df89a74d134e656c635cebcdfee62.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$\\frac{x^4-6x^3-40x^2+52x+6}{x^2+4x-8}$\" style=\"vertical-align: -15px\" width=\"213\" height=\"42\" /> after first performing the setup described above. Notice how <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/c/7/c/c7cab1a05e1e0c1d51a6a219d96577a16b7abf9d.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$4$\" style=\"vertical-align: -1px\" width=\"11\" height=\"14\" /> and <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/9/2/2/922c4f40c55a550006c796efcd63a7828ecfd95f.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$-8$\" width=\"22\" height=\"12\" /> become <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/7/2/d/72d85c6959aaabfff6e0104544ff25c1445f3f1e.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$-4$\" style=\"vertical-align: 0px\" width=\"23\" height=\"12\" /> and <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/1/2/a/12a9fb4c5b23667e57f28d844ad99715f18ba3fa.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$8,$\" style=\"vertical-align: -4px\" width=\"16\" height=\"17\" /> respectively, and how they are placed on the diagonal:\n</p><p><img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/8/b/8/8b8cfb1f9ca4fc9e7cd1f3bd7aa40dee9c6c9367.png\" class=\"latexcenter\" alt=\"[asy] draw((-1,-1)--(0,-1)--(0,0)--(1,0)--(1,1)); draw((2,-3.5)--(11,-3.5)); draw((7.5,1)--(7.5,-4.5),dotted);  label(&quot;$8$&quot;, (1,0), NW); label(&quot;$-4$&quot;, (0,-1), NW);  label(&quot;$1$&quot;, (2,0), NE); label(&quot;$-6$&quot;, (3.5,0), NE); label(&quot;$-40$&quot;, (5.25,0), NE); label(&quot;$52$&quot;, (7.75,0), NE); label(&quot;$6$&quot;, (10,0), NE); [/asy]\" width=\"252\" height=\"112\" />\n</p><p>Notice that the dotted line (which separates the quotient from the remainder) now has two terms to its right, because the remainder of dividing a polynomial by a factor of degree <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/4/1/c/41c544263a265ff15498ee45f7392c5f86c6d151.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$2$\" style=\"vertical-align: -1px\" width=\"11\" height=\"14\" /> will have degree <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/4/2/a/42a98fe777f28eaf12c11c90b1999e3b6575fa6a.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$2-1=1$\" style=\"vertical-align: -2px\" width=\"76\" height=\"15\" />, and so will be of the form <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/5/f/d/5fde213428f248a4e332fd531ac9fb6be859612a.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$Ax+B$\" style=\"vertical-align: -2px\" width=\"63\" height=\"16\" /> for constants <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/0/1/9/019e9892786e493964e145e7c5cf7b700314e53b.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$A$\" style=\"vertical-align: -1px\" width=\"15\" height=\"14\" /> and <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/f/f/5/ff5fb3d775862e2123b007eb4373ff6cc1a34d4e.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$B$\" style=\"vertical-align: -1px\" width=\"17\" height=\"14\" />.\n</p><p>Now, as we did for linear factors, we drop down the first coefficient:\n<img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/6/7/7/6771dbf94505b2e037a23d359c078e822be7fbea.png\" class=\"latexcenter\" alt=\"[asy] draw((-1,-1)--(0,-1)--(0,0)--(1,0)--(1,1)); draw((2,-3.5)--(11,-3.5)); draw((7.5,1)--(7.5,-4.5),dotted);  label(&quot;$8$&quot;, (1,0), NW); label(&quot;$-4$&quot;, (0,-1), NW);  label(&quot;$1$&quot;, (2,0), NE); label(&quot;$-6$&quot;, (3.5,0), NE); label(&quot;$-40$&quot;, (5.25,0), NE); label(&quot;$52$&quot;, (7.75,0), NE); label(&quot;$6$&quot;, (10,0), NE);  label(&quot;$1$&quot;, (2,-4.25), E); [/asy]\" width=\"252\" height=\"115\" />\n</p><p>Now, we multiply this first term below the bar by the diagonal (which, because this term equals <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/d/c/e/dce34f4dfb2406144304ad0d6106c5382ddd1446.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$1$\" style=\"vertical-align: -1px\" width=\"11\" height=\"14\" />, keeps the diagonal as it is) and then place this multiplied diagonal in the second and third columns:\n</p><p><img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/3/8/8/3887fc61cb7b2a303e082d743a98e7e3ce2c77d0.png\" class=\"latexcenter\" alt=\"[asy] draw((-1,-1)--(0,-1)--(0,0)--(1,0)--(1,1)); draw((2,-3.5)--(11,-3.5)); draw((7.5,1)--(7.5,-4.5),dotted);  label(&quot;$8$&quot;, (1,0), NW); label(&quot;$-4$&quot;, (0,-1), NW);  label(&quot;$1$&quot;, (2,0), NE); label(&quot;$-6$&quot;, (3.5,0), NE); label(&quot;$-40$&quot;, (5.25,0), NE); label(&quot;$52$&quot;, (7.75,0), NE); label(&quot;$6$&quot;, (10,0), NE);  label(&quot;$8$&quot;, (6, -1.25), E);  label(&quot;$-4$&quot;, (3.5, -2.5), E);  label(&quot;$1$&quot;, (2,-4.25), E); [/asy]\" width=\"252\" height=\"115\" />\n</p><p>Now, we add up all the numbers in the second column and place the resulting sum below the bar:\n</p><p><img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/4/5/b/45bde3f40075843cca69d94aa3c737e0b7a224dc.png\" class=\"latexcenter\" alt=\"[asy] draw((-1,-1)--(0,-1)--(0,0)--(1,0)--(1,1)); draw((2,-3.5)--(11,-3.5)); draw((7.5,1)--(7.5,-4.5),dotted);  label(&quot;$8$&quot;, (1,0), NW); label(&quot;$-4$&quot;, (0,-1), NW);  label(&quot;$1$&quot;, (2,0), NE); label(&quot;$-6$&quot;, (3.5,0), NE); label(&quot;$-40$&quot;, (5.25,0), NE); label(&quot;$52$&quot;, (7.75,0), NE); label(&quot;$6$&quot;, (10,0), NE);  label(&quot;$8$&quot;, (6, -1), E);  label(&quot;$-4$&quot;, (3.5, -2.5), E);  label(&quot;$1$&quot;, (2,-4.25), E); label(&quot;$-10$&quot;, (3.25, -4.25), E); [/asy]\" width=\"252\" height=\"115\" />\n</p><p>Now, we can multiply the diagonal by this second term below the bar, place this diagonal on the next two columns, add up the next column, and repeat until we have found all of the coefficients of the desired quotient and remainder. Note that once numbers are placed on the last column, no new multiplied diagonals should be placed:\n</p><p><img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/3/9/2/39279f8e9800e9db7df612f28cca620b49b67ad4.png\" class=\"latexcenter\" alt=\"[asy] size(7cm);  draw((-1,-1)--(0,-1)--(0,0)--(1,0)--(1,1)); draw((2,-3.5)--(11,-3.5)); draw((7.25,1)--(7.25,-4.5),dotted);  label(&quot;$8$&quot;, (1,0), NW); label(&quot;$-4$&quot;, (0,-1), NW);  label(&quot;$1$&quot;, (2,0), NE); label(&quot;$-6$&quot;, (3.5,0), NE); label(&quot;$-40$&quot;, (5.25,0), NE); label(&quot;$52$&quot;, (7.75,0), NE); label(&quot;$6$&quot;, (10,0), NE);  label(&quot;$8$&quot;, (6, -1), E); label(&quot;$-80$&quot;, (7.25, -1), E); label(&quot;$64$&quot;, (9.75, -1), E);  label(&quot;$-4$&quot;, (3.5, -2.5), E); label(&quot;$40$&quot;, (5.75,-2.5), E); label(&quot;$-32$&quot;, (7.25, -2.5), E);  label(&quot;$1$&quot;, (2,-4.25), E); label(&quot;$-10$&quot;, (3.25, -4.25), E); label(&quot;$8$&quot;, (6,-4.25), E); label(&quot;$-60$&quot;, (7.25, -4.25), E); label(&quot;$70$&quot;, (9.75, -4.25), E); [/asy]\" width=\"332\" height=\"155\" />\n</p><p>Note that because we are dividing a polynomial of degree <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/c/7/c/c7cab1a05e1e0c1d51a6a219d96577a16b7abf9d.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$4$\" style=\"vertical-align: -1px\" width=\"11\" height=\"14\" /> by one of degree <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/4/1/c/41c544263a265ff15498ee45f7392c5f86c6d151.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$2$\" style=\"vertical-align: -1px\" width=\"11\" height=\"14\" />, the quotient will have degree <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/f/0/1/f0119d2c68cde8369232a53eaff7ffc5c582b7ad.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$4-2=2$\" style=\"vertical-align: -2px\" width=\"76\" height=\"15\" />.\n</p><p>Now, we can use these coefficients we derived to deduce our final answer: <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/9/f/6/9f6567cbf2aa856f90d1d77a64018dfea0845ce7.png\" class=\"latexcenter\" alt=\"\\[\\frac{x^4-6x^3-40x^2+52x+6}{x^2+4x-8}=\\boxed{x^2-10x+8+\\frac{-60x+70}{x^2+4x-8}}.\\]\" width=\"465\" height=\"50\" />\n</p>\n<h3><span class=\"mw-headline\" id=\"Non-monic_Factors_2\">Non-monic Factors</span><span class=\"mw-editsection\"><span class=\"mw-editsection-bracket\">[</span><a href=\"/wiki/index.php?title=Synthetic_division&amp;action=edit&amp;section=10\" title=\"Edit section: Non-monic Factors\">edit</a><span class=\"mw-editsection-bracket\">]</span></span></h3>\n<p>Just like with linear factors, we can factor out the leading coefficient from the factor to transform the fraction into a constant factor times a fraction with a monic factor. For example: <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/e/1/a/e1a2f540b2ddda7d861b06651dc605f304310e5b.png\" class=\"latexcenter\" alt=\"\\[\\frac{x^3-4x+5}{4x^2+8x-12}=\\frac14\\cdot\\frac{x^3-4x+5}{x^2+2x-3}.\\]\" width=\"259\" height=\"41\" />\n</p><p><br />\n</p>\n<h3><span class=\"mw-headline\" id=\"Higher_Degree_Factors\">Higher Degree Factors</span><span class=\"mw-editsection\"><span class=\"mw-editsection-bracket\">[</span><a href=\"/wiki/index.php?title=Synthetic_division&amp;action=edit&amp;section=11\" title=\"Edit section: Higher Degree Factors\">edit</a><span class=\"mw-editsection-bracket\">]</span></span></h3>\n<p>For factors that are cubic or of higher degree, perform the same process with the opposites of the non-leading coefficients lined up in a diagonal from left to right. Once you cannot place any more full diagonals, do not place any more numbers, just as we stopped in the quadratic example above.\n</p>\n<h3><span class=\"mw-headline\" id=\"Problems\">Problems</span><span class=\"mw-editsection\"><span class=\"mw-editsection-bracket\">[</span><a href=\"/wiki/index.php?title=Synthetic_division&amp;action=edit&amp;section=12\" title=\"Edit section: Problems\">edit</a><span class=\"mw-editsection-bracket\">]</span></span></h3>\n<ol><li>Show that <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/a/3/9/a39839312dbcc5dee13988c127fd928dd193ba85.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$\\frac{2z^4+3}{2z^2-6z+8}=\\frac12\\left(2z^2+6z+10+\\frac{6z-37}{z^2-3z+4}\\right)$\" style=\"vertical-align: -18px\" width=\"392\" height=\"45\" />.</li>\n<li>Evaluate <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/5/3/4/534f1360a96fbd4bd15be53614e18afe73b59fbf.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$\\frac{x^5-3x^3+6x^2-20x+12}{x^2-x+2}$\" style=\"vertical-align: -15px\" width=\"213\" height=\"42\" />.</li>\n<li>Evaluate <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/a/0/b/a0b070945b0875c6318b1f1a258ad9075f436a9a.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$\\frac{(y-8)^3+3y^4+3y^2}{y^2+2y-1}$\" style=\"vertical-align: -17px\" width=\"165\" height=\"44\" />.</li></ol>\n<h2><span class=\"mw-headline\" id=\"See_Also\">See Also</span><span class=\"mw-editsection\"><span class=\"mw-editsection-bracket\">[</span><a href=\"/wiki/index.php?title=Synthetic_division&amp;action=edit&amp;section=13\" title=\"Edit section: See Also\">edit</a><span class=\"mw-editsection-bracket\">]</span></span></h2>\n<ul><li><a href=\"/wiki/index.php?title=Polynomial_long_division&amp;action=edit&amp;redlink=1\" class=\"new\" title=\"Polynomial long division (page does not exist)\">Polynomial long division</a></li>\n<li><a href=\"/wiki/index.php?title=Factor_Theorem\" title=\"Factor Theorem\">Factor Theorem</a></li>\n<li><a href=\"/wiki/index.php?title=Polynomial_Remainder_Theorem\" title=\"Polynomial Remainder Theorem\">Polynomial Remainder Theorem</a></li>\n<li><a href=\"/wiki/index.php?title=Rational_Root_Theorem\" class=\"mw-redirect\" title=\"Rational Root Theorem\">Rational Root Theorem</a></li></ul>\n<p><i>This article is a stub.  Help us out by <span class=\"plainlinks\"><a rel=\"nofollow\" class=\"external text\" href=\"https://artofproblemsolving.com/wiki/index.php?title=Synthetic_division&amp;action=edit\">expanding it</a></span>.</i>\n</p>\n<!-- \nNewPP limit report\nCached time: 20261018111537\nCache expiry: 86400\nReduced expiry: false\nComplications: [show‐toc]\nCPU time usage: 0.042 seconds\nReal time usage: 0.058 seconds\nPreprocessor visited node count: 441/1000000\nPost‐expand include size: 295/2097152 bytes\nTemplate argument size: 0/2097152 bytes\nHighest expansion depth: 4/100\nExpensive parser function count: 0/100\nUnstrip recursion depth: 0/20\nUnstrip post‐expand size: 20369/5000000 bytes\n-->\n<!--\nTransclusion expansion time report (%,ms,calls,template)\n100.00%    5.843      1 Template:Stub\n100.00%    5.843      1 -total\n-->\n\n<!-- Saved in parser cache with key wiki-wiki_:pcache:31161:|#|:idhash:canonical and timestamp 20261018111537 and revision id 244781. Rendering was triggered because: api-parse\n -->\n</div>"}